Synthesized comprehensive SDR monitoring framework for anomalous Bluetooth detection

Here's what you've got:

ble_capture.cpp — C++ engine that drives the HackRF at 4 Msps across all three BLE advertising channels (2402/2426/2480 MHz) with 80ms dwell time per hop. It runs a real FM discriminator for GFSK demodulation, correlates against the BLE advertising access address (0x8E89BED6), applies channel de-whitening, validates CRC-24, and emits structured NDJSON to stdout. No transmit, no scan requests — purely passive IQ capture and demod.

ble_analyzer.py — Python engine that consumes the packet stream and runs 16 anomaly detection checks across four categories. The ones most relevant to your 250-device scenario are FLD-001 (MAC flood — flags when >100 unique addresses appear in a 60s window), FLD-003 (burst clusters — catches coordinated mass-appearance of new MACs), GHO-002 (coordinated ghost clusters — devices that appear for under 5 seconds then vanish, especially when multiple do it simultaneously), and JAM-001/JAM-002 (broadband or partial jamming where energy is present but packets don't decode). Everything writes to a BLAKE3 hash-chained evidence file compatible with your SDAR architecture.

Key design decision: The pipeline separates the time-critical RF path (C++) from the analysis path (Python) via a simple NDJSON pipe. This means you can also capture raw NDJSON to disk with tee and replay later for re-analysis with different thresholds, or cross-correlate between both HackRFs using the make run-dual target.

When the SM-G930FDs arrive on the 16th with NetHunter, you can run the HackRFs as the ground-truth SDR capture layer while the phones provide an independent hci-level BLE scan for correlation — two completely independent observation methods documenting the same anomaly.
